== Below is an real-world example of factual background from a difference case, from which you can learn the writting techniques, style and tones:

Plaintiff Dave McLaughlin (“Plaintiff”) is a real estate agent in Agoura Hills, California, who filed this action against Defendant HomeLight, Inc. (“HomeLight” or “Defendant”) for false advertising under the Lanham Act, 15 U.S.C. § 1125(a). (First Am. Compl. (“FAC”) ¶¶ 1, 7.) HomeLight is a technology company that, among other services, operates an online real estate referral platform connecting consumers with real estate agents through a proprietary algorithm that considers numerous data points, including property transaction history and client reviews. (Request for Judicial Notice (“RJN”), Exs. 1–3; FAC ¶ 8(F).)

HomeLight has operated in Agoura Hills, California, since 2012. (RJN, Ex. 4.) As part of its service, HomeLight’s website invites potential home buyers or sellers to submit details about their particular real estate goals—such as home type, price range, and neighborhood—via an online form. (RJN, Ex. 5.) HomeLight’s proprietary algorithm then uses quantitative and qualitative metrics to generate individualized, or “custom,” agent recommendations, highlighting agents it deems a suitable “match” for the specific consumer. (FAC ¶ 8(F).) HomeLight’s website also features landing pages for specific geographic areas, including one for Agoura Hills (the “Agoura Hills Landing Page”). (FAC ¶¶ 8(A)–(F).)

Plaintiff challenges certain statements that appeared on or around early 2021 on the Agoura Hills Landing Page, which describe HomeLight’s methodology and purportedly identify top real estate agents based on transaction volume and performance metrics. (FAC ¶ 8.) Specifically, Plaintiff alleges these statements are “false on their face,” including:

Method Statements. Plaintiff alleges HomeLight falsely states that it “analyzes millions of home sales to find the best performing real estate agents” (FAC ¶ 8(A)); “runs the stats on each of the 1,565 agents in Agoura Hills so you can select the top performing real estate agents” (FAC ¶ 8(B)); and “analyzes all real estate agents in Agoura Hills to bring [consumers] custom, unbiased, data-driven recommendations,” allegedly using “actual home sales transaction data” (FAC ¶ 8(F)).

Top Real Estate Agent Descriptions. Plaintiff points to HomeLight’s various references to the “top 3%” of Agoura Hills agents who purportedly “work with 611 homes and get them sold in 44 days” (FAC ¶ 8(B)), or similar statements comparing “top agents” to “average” agents regarding the speed, number of properties, and percentage price savings or gains for consumers. (FAC ¶¶ 8(D), 8(AA), 8(CC), 8(DD).)

Top Real Estate Agent Recommendations. The FAC also challenges the “See how 20 of the top real estate agents in Agoura Hills, CA stack up” banner, followed by profiles of certain named real estate agents and purported transaction data for each. (FAC ¶¶ 8(C), 8(BB).)

Compensation Representations. Finally, Plaintiff alleges that HomeLight falsely represents that these recommended agents “do not pay” to appear on the platform or to receive referrals. (FAC ¶ 8(D).)

According to Plaintiff, he conducted more real estate transactions in Agoura Hills during the relevant period than some of the agents identified on HomeLight’s website, yet he was not included in the “Top Agent” rankings. (FAC ¶¶ 9, 10.) Plaintiff alleges that HomeLight’s statements describing its methodology and ranking results are false or misleading, particularly because, in his view, certain listed agents had few or no recent transactions in Agoura Hills, while Plaintiff had significant sales volume. (FAC ¶¶ 8–9.) Plaintiff further alleges that HomeLight’s business model is a “pay-for-play” referral system and that consumers are misled into believing the listed agents have superior qualifications based on objective, data-driven measures. (FAC ¶¶ 9, 16.)

Plaintiff seeks damages in excess of $500,000, attorneys’ fees, punitive damages, and an injunction prohibiting HomeLight from making the challenged statements. (FAC ¶ 19.) HomeLight now moves to dismiss the First Amended Complaint pursuant to Rule 12(b)(6) of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure.
